<section class="chapter" id="chapter-1">
<h2>
                        1. Understanding Operating System Resources
                    </h2>
<p>
                        An operating system resource is any
                        hardware or software component required
                        by a process to perform its operations.
                    </p>
<h3>Hardware Resources</h3>
<ul>
<li>Central Processing Unit (CPU)</li>
<li>Random Access Memory (RAM)</li>
<li>Hard Disk Drive (HDD)</li>
<li>Solid State Drive (SSD)</li>
<li>Printers and scanners</li>
<li>Network adapters</li>
</ul>
<h3>Software Resources</h3>
<ul>
<li>Files</li>
<li>Database records</li>
<li>Software locks</li>
<li>Application permissions</li>
<li>Communication channels</li>
</ul>
<h3>Shared Resources</h3>
<p>
                        Shared resources can be accessed by multiple
                        processes according to established access
                        rules. For example, several programs may
                        read a file, but writing may require
                        exclusive access.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-2">
<h2>
                        2. Introduction to Processes
                    </h2>
<p>
                        A process is a program currently being
                        executed. Unlike a stored program, a
                        process actively uses system resources.
                    </p>
<h3>Components of a Process</h3>
<ul>
<li>Process ID</li>
<li>Program counter</li>
<li>CPU registers</li>
<li>Memory space</li>
<li>Process state</li>
<li>Open files</li>
</ul>
<h3>Process States</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>New:</strong>
                            Being created.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Ready:</strong>
                            Waiting for CPU time.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Running:</strong>
                            Executing instructions.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Waiting:</strong>
                            Waiting for a resource or event.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Terminated:</strong>
                            Finished execution.
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
