<section class="chapter" id="chapter-3">
<h2>
                        3. Definition and Characteristics of Deadlock
                    </h2>
<p>
                        A deadlock is a condition where two or
                        more processes remain blocked because
                        each process is waiting for a resource
                        held by another process.
                    </p>
<h3>Main Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processes are blocked.</li>
<li>Resources remain allocated.</li>
<li>Processes wait for one another.</li>
<li>No affected process can continue.</li>
<li>System productivity is reduced.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            Computer A holds a printer and needs
                            a scanner. Computer B holds the scanner
                            and needs the printer.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Neither can proceed while both continue
                            holding their resources.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-4">
<h2>
                        4. Understanding Resource Allocation
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Resource allocation is the process of
                        assigning available computing resources
                        to processes that require them.
                    </p>
<h3>Five Stages</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Request:</strong>
                            A process requests a resource.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Evaluation:</strong>
                            The operating system checks availability.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Assignment:</strong>
                            The resource is allocated.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Usage:</strong>
                            The process uses the resource.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Release:</strong>
                            The resource becomes available again.
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-5">
<h2>
                        5. Resource Allocation Methods
                    </h2>
<h3>Static Resource Allocation</h3>
<p>
                        Resources are assigned before process
                        execution. This makes allocation simpler,
                        but may leave resources unused.
                    </p>
<h3>Dynamic Resource Allocation</h3>
<p>
                        Resources are allocated while processes
                        are running. This provides greater
                        flexibility but requires more complex
                        resource management.
                    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Static</th>
<th>Dynamic</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Allocation Time</td>
<td>Before execution</td>
<td>During execution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Flexibility</td>
<td>Lower</td>
<td>Higher</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Management</td>
<td>Simpler</td>
<td>More complex</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
