<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 7
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-7">
<h2>
                        7. How Deadlocks Occur
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Deadlocks develop when processes request
                        resources in an order that creates
                        circular dependencies.
                    </p>
<ol>
<li>
                            Process A receives Resource 1.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Process B receives Resource 2.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Process A requests Resource 2.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Process B requests Resource 1.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Both processes continue waiting.
                        </li>
</ol>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Important:</strong>
<p>
                            Not every waiting process is deadlocked.
                            Normal waiting may end when another
                            process releases a resource.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 8
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-8">
<h2>
                        8. Four Necessary Conditions of Deadlock
                    </h2>
<p>
                        The four Coffman conditions must exist
                        simultaneously for deadlock in the
                        traditional resource-deadlock model.
                    </p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Mutual Exclusion:</strong>
                            At least one resource cannot be shared.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Hold and Wait:</strong>
                            A process holds a resource while
                            requesting another.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>No Preemption:</strong>
                            Resources cannot be forcibly removed.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Circular Wait:</strong>
                            Processes form a cycle of dependencies.
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 9
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-9">
<h2>
                        9. Mutual Exclusion and Hold and Wait
                    </h2>
<h3>Mutual Exclusion</h3>
<p>
                        Mutual exclusion is a condition in which
                        a resource can be used by only one
                        process at a time.
                    </p>
<p>
                        When a process is using an exclusive
                        resource, other processes requesting
                        that resource must wait until it
                        becomes available.
                    </p>
<h3>Examples of Mutual Exclusion</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            A printer handling one print job
                            at a time.
                        </li>
<li>
                            A process holding an exclusive
                            file lock.
                        </li>
<li>
                            A thread holding a mutex while
                            modifying shared information.
                        </li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Process A is using a printer.
                            Process B also needs the printer,
                            but it must wait until Process A
                            releases the resource.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Hold and Wait</h3>
<p>
                        Hold and wait occurs when a process
                        holds at least one resource while
                        waiting to acquire another resource.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This can create a deadlock when
                        multiple processes hold resources
                        that other processes need.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Process A holds Resource 1
                            and requests Resource 2.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Process B holds Resource 2
                            and requests Resource 1.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Neither process can continue
                            because both are waiting for
                            resources held by the other.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Difference Between the Two Conditions</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Condition</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Mutual Exclusion</td>
<td>
                                        A resource can be used
                                        by only one process at a time.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hold and Wait</td>
<td>
                                        A process holds resources
                                        while requesting additional ones.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Understanding these conditions helps
                        operating system designers identify
                        resource conflicts and develop methods
                        for preventing deadlocks.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 10
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-10">
<h2>
                        10. No Preemption and Circular Wait
                    </h2>
<h3>No Preemption</h3>
<p>
                        No preemption means that a resource
                        cannot be forcibly taken away from
                        a process while it is using it.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The process must release the resource
                        voluntarily after completing its task.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Process A holds an exclusive
                            resource that Process B needs.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If the operating system cannot
                            safely remove that resource
                            from Process A, Process B
                            must wait.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Circular Wait</h3>
<p>
                        Circular wait occurs when a group
                        of processes forms a circular chain,
                        with each process waiting for
                        a resource held by the next process.
                    </p>
<h3>Example of Circular Wait</h3>
<div class="example-box">
<p>
                            Consider three processes:
                        </p>
<ul>
<li>
                                Process A waits for a resource
                                held by Process B.
                            </li>
<li>
                                Process B waits for a resource
                                held by Process C.
                            </li>
<li>
                                Process C waits for a resource
                                held by Process A.
                            </li>
</ul>
<p>
                            This creates a circular dependency
                            that prevents the processes
                            from continuing.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Comparison</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Condition</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>No Preemption</td>
<td>
                                        A resource cannot be
                                        forcibly removed from
                                        its current holder.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Circular Wait</td>
<td>
                                        Processes form a cycle
                                        of resource dependencies.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Breaking either of these conditions
                        can help prevent deadlocks.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 11
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-11">
<h2>
                        11. Resource Allocation Graph
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is a Resource Allocation Graph?</h3>
<p>
                        A Resource Allocation Graph (RAG)
                        is a graphical representation
                        of the relationships between
                        processes and resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It helps identify which resources
                        are allocated to processes
                        and which resources are being requested.
                    </p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<h3>1. Process Nodes</h3>
<p>
                        A process is represented by a circle.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<p>
                            Example: P1, P2, and P3
                            represent different processes.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>2. Resource Nodes</h3>
<p>
                        A resource type is represented
                        by a rectangle.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<p>
                            Example: R1 and R2 represent
                            different resource types.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>3. Request Edge</h3>
<p>
                        A request edge points from
                        a process to a resource.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It indicates that the process
                        is requesting the resource.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<pre><code>P1 → R1</code></pre>
<p>
                            Process P1 is requesting
                            Resource R1.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>4. Assignment Edge</h3>
<p>
                        An assignment edge points from
                        a resource instance to a process.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It indicates that the resource
                        has been allocated to the process.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<pre><code>R1 → P1</code></pre>
<p>
                            Resource R1 is assigned
                            to Process P1.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Interpreting the Graph</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            A request edge indicates
                            a process is waiting for a resource.
                        </li>
<li>
                            An assignment edge indicates
                            that a process holds a resource.
                        </li>
<li>
                            A cycle may indicate
                            a possible deadlock.
                        </li>
<li>
                            If every resource type has
                            only one instance, a cycle
                            indicates deadlock.
                        </li>
<li>
                            If resource types have multiple
                            instances, a cycle alone
                            does not prove deadlock.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Resource Allocation Graphs help
                        visualize resource dependencies
                        and understand how deadlocks
                        can develop.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 12
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-12">
<h2>
                        12. Resource Allocation Graph Examples
                    </h2>
<h3>Example 1: No Deadlock</h3>
<p>
                        Consider two processes and
                        one resource.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<pre><code>R1 → P1

P2 → R1</code></pre>
<p>
                            Resource R1 is assigned to P1.
                            Process P2 is requesting R1.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If P1 finishes and releases R1,
                            P2 can obtain the resource.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Therefore, this situation
                            does not necessarily represent
                            a deadlock.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Example 2: Deadlock</h3>
<p>
                        Consider two processes and
                        two single-instance resources.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<pre><code>R1 → P1
P1 → R2

R2 → P2
P2 → R1</code></pre>
<p>
                            Process P1 holds R1
                            and requests R2.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Process P2 holds R2
                            and requests R1.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Because each resource has
                            only one instance and neither
                            process can continue,
                            a deadlock exists.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Example 3: Multiple Resource Instances</h3>
<p>
                        When a resource type contains
                        multiple instances, the presence
                        of a cycle does not always
                        indicate a deadlock.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<p>
                            Suppose R1 has two instances.
                            One instance is held by P1,
                            while another is held by P3.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If P3 can complete its task
                            and release its instance,
                            another waiting process
                            may continue.
                        </p>
<p>
                            The cycle may therefore
                            be resolved without deadlock.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Important Reminder</h3>
<p>
                        A cycle in a Resource Allocation Graph
                        is a necessary condition for deadlock,
                        but with multiple resource instances,
                        it is not always sufficient
                        to confirm deadlock.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 13
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-13">
<h2>
                        13. Deadlock vs. Starvation vs. Livelock
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Deadlock, starvation, and livelock
                        are different problems that can
                        affect process execution.
                    </p>
<h3>1. Deadlock</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock occurs when processes
                        are permanently blocked while
                        waiting for resources held
                        by one another.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            Process A waits for a resource
                            held by Process B, while
                            Process B waits for a resource
                            held by Process A.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>2. Starvation</h3>
<p>
                        Starvation occurs when a process
                        waits indefinitely because
                        other processes repeatedly
                        receive the resources it needs.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            A low-priority process may
                            continuously wait because
                            higher-priority processes
                            are repeatedly scheduled first.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>3. Livelock</h3>
<p>
                        Livelock occurs when processes
                        continue changing their states
                        or responding to one another
                        without making useful progress.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            Two processes repeatedly
                            release and request resources
                            to avoid conflict, but
                            neither completes its task.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Comparison</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Problem</th>
<th>Behavior</th>
<th>Progress</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Deadlock</td>
<td>
                                        Processes remain blocked.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        No progress.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Starvation</td>
<td>
                                        A process waits indefinitely.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        Other processes may progress.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Livelock</td>
<td>
                                        Processes remain active.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        No useful progress.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Understanding these differences
                        helps developers select suitable
                        synchronization and scheduling
                        strategies.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 14
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-14">
<h2>
                        14. Methods for Handling Deadlocks
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Operating systems can use different
                        strategies to manage deadlocks
                        and reduce their effects.
                    </p>
<h3>1. Deadlock Prevention</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock prevention ensures that
                        at least one of the four necessary
                        deadlock conditions cannot occur.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Deadlock Avoidance</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock avoidance evaluates
                        resource requests before granting
                        them to help maintain a safe state.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Deadlock Detection</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock detection allows resource
                        allocation to proceed and checks
                        whether deadlocks have occurred.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Deadlock Recovery</h3>
<p>
                        Recovery methods attempt to
                        resolve a detected deadlock
                        by terminating processes,
                        rolling back operations,
                        or reclaiming resources.
                    </p>
<h3>5. Ignoring Deadlocks</h3>
<p>
                        Some systems do not actively
                        prevent or detect every
                        possible deadlock.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This approach may be selected
                        when deadlocks are uncommon
                        and the cost of prevention
                        is considered too high.
                    </p>
<h3>Comparison of Strategies</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>Main Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Prevention</td>
<td>
                                        Eliminate a necessary
                                        deadlock condition.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Avoidance</td>
<td>
                                        Keep the system
                                        in a safe state.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Detection</td>
<td>
                                        Identify existing deadlocks.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recovery</td>
<td>
                                        Resolve detected deadlocks.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ignore</td>
<td>
                                        Accept the possibility
                                        of deadlock.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 15
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-15">
<h2>
                        15. Deadlock Prevention Techniques
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Deadlock prevention uses resource
                        management rules to ensure that
                        at least one of the four necessary
                        deadlock conditions cannot occur.
                    </p>
<h3>1. Preventing Mutual Exclusion</h3>
<p>
                        When possible, resources can
                        be designed for sharing.
                    </p>
<p>
                        For example, multiple processes
                        may read the same file without
                        requiring exclusive access.
                    </p>
<p>
                        However, some resources cannot
                        be safely shared.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Preventing Hold and Wait</h3>
<p>
                        Processes may be required
                        to request all necessary
                        resources before execution.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Another approach requires
                        processes to release currently
                        held resources before requesting
                        additional resources.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Preventing No Preemption</h3>
<p>
                        Some resources may be reclaimed
                        from processes when they cannot
                        obtain additional resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This approach is suitable only
                        when resources can be safely
                        preempted or their state restored.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Preventing Circular Wait</h3>
<p>
                        Resources can be assigned
                        a fixed ordering.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Processes must request resources
                        according to that order.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Suppose resources are ordered:
                        </p>
<pre><code>R1 → R2 → R3</code></pre>
<p>
                            A process must request resources
                            in increasing order.
                        </p>
<p>
                            This prevents processes from
                            creating circular resource
                            dependencies.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Prevents deadlocks before they occur.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Provides predictable resource
                            management rules.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Reduces the need for deadlock recovery.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            May reduce resource utilization.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Can limit process flexibility.
                        </li>
<li>
                            May increase waiting times.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 16
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-16">
<h2>
                        16. Deadlock Avoidance Techniques
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is Deadlock Avoidance?</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock avoidance is a resource
                        allocation strategy that evaluates
                        requests before granting them.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system grants
                        a request only when doing so
                        allows the system to remain
                        in a safe state.
                    </p>
<h3>Safe State</h3>
<p>
                        A safe state is a condition
                        where there is at least one
                        sequence in which all processes
                        can finish successfully.
                    </p>
<h3>Unsafe State</h3>
<p>
                        An unsafe state is a condition
                        where the system cannot guarantee
                        that all processes will finish.
                    </p>
<p>
                        An unsafe state does not always
                        mean that deadlock has already occurred.
                    </p>
<h3>Common Avoidance Methods</h3>
<h3>1. Resource Allocation Graph Algorithm</h3>
<p>
                        This method can be used when
                        each resource type has
                        a single instance.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It examines potential allocations
                        to avoid creating unsafe
                        resource dependencies.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Banker's Algorithm</h3>
<p>
                        The Banker's Algorithm evaluates
                        available resources, allocated
                        resources, and maximum process demands.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It determines whether granting
                        a request would leave the
                        system in a safe state.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Helps prevent the system
                            from entering unsafe states.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Allows more flexible allocation
                            than some prevention methods.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Supports systematic resource
                            request evaluation.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Often requires advance knowledge
                            of maximum resource demands.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Adds computational overhead.
                        </li>
<li>
                            May delay requests that could
                            otherwise be granted immediately.
                        </li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            A process requests an additional
                            memory-related resource.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Before granting the request,
                            the system checks whether
                            the remaining resources
                            would still allow all
                            processes to complete.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If the resulting state is unsafe,
                            the request is delayed.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     CHAPTER 17
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-17">
<h2>
                        17. Safe and Unsafe States
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is a Safe State?</h3>
<p>
                        A safe state is a condition in which
                        the operating system can allocate
                        resources to processes in an order
                        that allows all processes to complete
                        their execution successfully.
                    </p>
<p>
                        In a safe state, there is at least
                        one sequence of processes that
                        guarantees successful completion
                        under their declared maximum
                        resource requirements.
                    </p>
<h3>Safe Sequence</h3>
<p>
                        A safe sequence is an ordering of
                        processes in which each process
                        can obtain the resources it still
                        needs, finish execution, and release
                        its allocated resources for
                        other processes.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example of a Safe Sequence</h3>
<p>
                            Consider three processes:
                        </p>
<pre><code>P1 → P2 → P3</code></pre>
<p>
                            If P1 can finish using the
                            currently available resources,
                            it releases its allocated resources.
                        </p>
<p>
                            P2 can then use the newly available
                            resources and finish.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Finally, P3 can complete
                            its execution.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Because all processes can finish,
                            the system is in a safe state.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>What Is an Unsafe State?</h3>
<p>
                        An unsafe state occurs when the system
                        cannot identify a safe sequence
                        that guarantees completion of
                        all processes.
                    </p>
<p>
                        An unsafe state does not necessarily
                        mean that a deadlock already exists.
                        However, deadlock may occur depending
                        on future resource requests.
                    </p>
<h3>Comparison of Safe and Unsafe States</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Safe State</th>
<th>Unsafe State</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
                                        At least one safe
                                        sequence exists.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        No safe sequence
                                        can be identified.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
                                        All processes can
                                        complete under the
                                        safety assumptions.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        Completion of every
                                        process cannot
                                        be guaranteed.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
                                        The system can avoid
                                        deadlock by following
                                        a safe allocation order.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        Deadlock is possible
                                        but not inevitable.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Understanding safe and unsafe states
                        helps operating systems determine
                        whether granting resource requests
                        could create dangerous allocation
                        conditions.
                    </p>
</section>
