<section class="chapter" id="chapter-18">
<h2>
                        18. Banker's Algorithm
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is the Banker's Algorithm?</h3>
<p>
                        The Banker's Algorithm is a deadlock
                        avoidance algorithm used to determine
                        whether allocating resources to
                        processes will leave the system
                        in a safe state.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It was developed by Edsger W. Dijkstra
                        and is named after the way a bank
                        manages its available funds while
                        considering customers' maximum
                        possible demands.
                    </p>
<h3>Purpose of the Algorithm</h3>
<p>
                        The algorithm evaluates resource
                        requests before granting them.
                        It attempts to ensure that enough
                        resources remain available for
                        all processes to eventually finish.
                    </p>
<h3>Important Data Structures</h3>
<h3>1. Available</h3>
<p>
                        Available represents the number
                        of currently available instances
                        of each resource type.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Maximum</h3>
<p>
                        Maximum represents the greatest
                        number of resource instances
                        each process may request.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Allocation</h3>
<p>
                        Allocation represents the number
                        of resource instances currently
                        assigned to each process.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Need</h3>
<p>
                        Need represents the additional
                        resources a process may still
                        require to finish.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre><code>Need = Maximum - Allocation</code></pre>
<p>
                            The calculation is performed
                            separately for every resource type
                            and process.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Steps of the Safety Algorithm</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                            Initialize Work with the
                            currently Available resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Set Finish to false for
                            all unfinished processes.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Find a process whose remaining
                            Need is less than or equal
                            to Work for every resource type.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Assume that process finishes
                            and releases its Allocation.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Add its Allocation to Work
                            and mark Finish as true.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Repeat until every process
                            finishes or no eligible
                            process can be found.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Determining the Result</h3>
<p>
                        If all processes can be marked
                        as finished, the system is safe.
                    </p>
<p>
                        If some processes cannot finish,
                        the system is unsafe.
                    </p>
<h3>Resource Request Algorithm</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                            Check whether the requested
                            resources are within the
                            process's remaining Need.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Check whether the requested
                            resources are currently Available.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Temporarily allocate the resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Run the safety algorithm.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Grant the request only if
                            the resulting state is safe.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Otherwise, restore the previous
                            allocation and make the process wait.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Helps avoid unsafe resource allocations.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Supports multiple resource types.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Provides a systematic method
                            for evaluating requests.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Maximum resource requirements
                            must be known in advance.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Resource availability must
                            be tracked accurately.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Repeated safety checks create
                            computational overhead.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
