<section class="chapter" id="chapter-20">
<h2>
                        20. Deadlock Detection
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is Deadlock Detection?</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock detection is the process
                        of examining resource allocation
                        and process dependencies to determine
                        whether a deadlock already exists.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Unlike prevention and avoidance,
                        detection allows the system to
                        enter a deadlocked state before
                        attempting to resolve it.
                    </p>
<h3>Detection Using a Wait-For Graph</h3>
<p>
                        A Wait-For Graph represents processes
                        as nodes and waiting relationships
                        as directed edges.
                    </p>
<p>
                        An edge from P1 to P2 means that
                        P1 is waiting for a resource
                        held by P2.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>P1 → P2
P2 → P3
P3 → P1</code></pre>
<p>
                            The graph contains a cycle.
                        </p>
<p>
                            In a system with single-instance
                            resources, this cycle indicates
                            a deadlock.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Detection with Multiple Resource Instances</h3>
<p>
                        When resources have multiple instances,
                        a more detailed detection algorithm
                        is required.
                    </p>
<h3>Important Data Structures</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Available:</strong>
                            Currently available resource instances.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Allocation:</strong>
                            Resources assigned to each process.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Request:</strong>
                            Additional resources each
                            process is currently waiting for.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Basic Detection Procedure</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                            Record the currently available resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Identify the resources allocated
                            to each process.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Examine outstanding resource requests.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Determine which processes
                            can complete.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Simulate the release of resources
                            from completed processes.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Repeat until no additional
                            process can finish.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Identify unfinished processes
                            that remain deadlocked.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Allows flexible resource allocation.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Can identify existing deadlocks.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Supports targeted recovery actions.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Detection requires computational resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Processes may remain blocked
                            before detection occurs.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Recovery may interrupt
                            running applications.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
