<section class="chapter" id="chapter-21">
<h2>
                        21. Deadlock Recovery
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is Deadlock Recovery?</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock recovery refers to the
                        methods used to restore system
                        progress after a deadlock
                        has been detected.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system must break
                        the resource dependency cycle
                        so that blocked processes
                        can continue.
                    </p>
<h3>Common Recovery Methods</h3>
<h3>1. Process Termination</h3>
<p>
                        One or more deadlocked processes
                        are terminated to release
                        their allocated resources.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Resource Preemption</h3>
<p>
                        Resources are taken from
                        selected processes when
                        safe preemption is possible.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Process Rollback</h3>
<p>
                        A process is returned to
                        a previously saved state.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This method requires appropriate
                        checkpointing or transaction
                        recovery support.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Restarting Processes</h3>
<p>
                        Processes may be restarted
                        after resources have
                        been released.
                    </p>
<h3>Factors to Consider During Recovery</h3>
<ul>
<li>Process priority</li>
<li>Resources currently allocated</li>
<li>Amount of completed work</li>
<li>Estimated remaining execution time</li>
<li>Cost of restarting the process</li>
<li>Impact on users and applications</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Two processes are deadlocked
                            because each holds a resource
                            needed by the other.
                        </p>
<p>
                            The operating system terminates
                            one process, releasing its resources.
                        </p>
<p>
                            The remaining process can
                            then obtain the resources
                            it needs and continue.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock recovery helps restore
                        normal system operations and
                        reduces the duration of
                        resource-related interruptions.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
