<section class="chapter" id="chapter-22">
<h2>
                        22. Resource Preemption and Process Termination
                    </h2>
<h3>Resource Preemption</h3>
<p>
                        Resource preemption is a recovery
                        technique in which a resource
                        is removed from a process
                        and reassigned when this
                        can be performed safely.
                    </p>
<h3>Important Considerations</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Victim Selection:</strong>
                            Choose the process or resource
                            whose preemption causes
                            the lowest recovery cost.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Rollback:</strong>
                            Restore the affected process
                            to a valid previous state
                            when necessary.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Starvation Prevention:</strong>
                            Avoid repeatedly selecting
                            the same process as the victim.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Process Termination</h3>
<p>
                        Process termination resolves
                        a deadlock by stopping
                        one or more processes.
                    </p>
<h3>Termination Strategies</h3>
<h3>1. Terminate All Deadlocked Processes</h3>
<p>
                        Every process involved in the
                        deadlock is terminated.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This approach can resolve
                        the deadlock quickly but
                        may result in significant
                        loss of completed work.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Terminate One Process at a Time</h3>
<p>
                        The operating system terminates
                        selected processes individually
                        until the deadlock is removed.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This approach may reduce
                        unnecessary termination but
                        requires repeated detection checks.
                    </p>
<h3>Comparison</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Possible Disadvantage</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Resource Preemption</td>
<td>
                                        Reclaims selected resources.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        May require rollback
                                        and careful state management.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Process Termination</td>
<td>
                                        Stops selected processes.
                                    </td>
<td>
                                        May cause loss
                                        of completed work.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            A process holds a resource
                            required by another process.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If the resource can be safely
                            reclaimed, the operating system
                            may use preemption.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Otherwise, terminating
                            a selected process may
                            be necessary to restore progress.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
