<section class="chapter" id="chapter-23">
<h2>
                        23. Deadlocks in Database Systems
                    </h2>
<h3>What Is a Database Deadlock?</h3>
<p>
                        A database deadlock occurs when
                        two or more transactions are
                        waiting for locks held by
                        one another, preventing
                        the transactions from continuing.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Database management systems
                        use locks to maintain
                        data consistency when
                        multiple transactions
                        access shared records.
                    </p>
<h3>Example of a Database Deadlock</h3>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Transaction A</h3>
<ol>
<li>Locks Record 1.</li>
<li>Requests Record 2.</li>
<li>Waits for Transaction B.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Transaction B</h3>
<ol>
<li>Locks Record 2.</li>
<li>Requests Record 1.</li>
<li>Waits for Transaction A.</li>
</ol>
<p>
                            Both transactions remain
                            blocked because each holds
                            a lock required by the other.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Common Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Transactions acquire locks
                            in different orders.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Transactions hold locks
                            for long periods.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Multiple transactions update
                            shared records concurrently.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Poorly organized transaction
                            operations create circular waits.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Database Deadlock Prevention</h3>
<h3>1. Consistent Lock Ordering</h3>
<p>
                        Transactions should acquire
                        shared resources in a
                        consistent order.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Short Transactions</h3>
<p>
                        Transactions should avoid
                        holding locks longer
                        than necessary.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Appropriate Indexing</h3>
<p>
                        Suitable database indexes
                        can reduce unnecessary
                        scanning and may decrease
                        lock contention.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Deadlock Detection</h3>
<p>
                        Some database systems automatically
                        detect deadlocks and select
                        a transaction to roll back.
                    </p>
<h3>5. Transaction Retry</h3>
<p>
                        Applications can retry
                        transactions that were
                        rolled back because
                        of a deadlock.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Retry operations should be
                        designed carefully to avoid
                        duplicate effects.
                    </p>
<h3>Example: Banking Database</h3>
<div class="example-box">
<p>
                            Two transactions attempt
                            to transfer money between
                            the same bank accounts.
                        </p>
<p>
                            If the transactions lock
                            account records in different
                            orders, a deadlock may occur.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Using a consistent locking
                            order helps reduce the
                            risk of deadlock.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Understanding database deadlocks
                        is important for developers
                        building applications that
                        manage shared records,
                        financial transactions,
                        inventory systems,
                        and online services.
                    </p>
</section>
