<section class="chapter" id="chapter-24">
<h2>
                        24. Real-Life Applications of Deadlock Management
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Deadlock management is important in
                        real-world computer systems where
                        multiple processes or users compete
                        for shared resources.
                    </p>
<h3>1. Banking Systems</h3>
<p>
                        Banking applications process many
                        transactions simultaneously.
                        Transactions may need exclusive
                        access to account records to
                        maintain consistency.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Transaction A locks Account 1
                            and requests Account 2.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Transaction B locks Account 2
                            and requests Account 1.
                        </p>
<p>
                            A deadlock occurs when both
                            transactions wait for each
                            other to release their locks.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>2. Database Management Systems</h3>
<p>
                        Database systems use locking
                        mechanisms to control concurrent
                        access to shared records.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Deadlock detection and transaction
                        rollback can help restore progress
                        when conflicting transactions
                        become blocked.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Operating Systems</h3>
<p>
                        Operating systems manage resources
                        such as memory, files, devices,
                        and synchronization locks.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Proper resource allocation reduces
                        the risk of processes waiting
                        indefinitely for one another.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Web Servers</h3>
<p>
                        Web servers handle requests from
                        multiple users and may access
                        shared files, databases,
                        and application resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Consistent locking practices and
                        resource timeouts can help reduce
                        concurrency problems.
                    </p>
<h3>5. Cloud Computing</h3>
<p>
                        Cloud platforms distribute
                        computing resources among
                        applications and virtual machines.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Resource management helps prevent
                        conflicts and supports reliable
                        service delivery.
                    </p>
<h3>6. Printing Systems</h3>
<p>
                        Multiple applications may submit
                        documents to the same printer.
                    </p>
<p>
                        A print spooler manages print
                        requests so that applications
                        do not need to hold exclusive
                        access to the printer while
                        waiting for other resources.
                    </p>
<h3>7. Multithreaded Applications</h3>
<p>
                        Applications may contain several
                        threads that access shared
                        data structures.
                    </p>
<p>
                        If threads acquire multiple
                        locks in inconsistent orders,
                        deadlocks can occur.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                            Thread A holds Lock 1 and
                            waits for Lock 2.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Thread B holds Lock 2 and
                            waits for Lock 1.
                        </p>
<p>
                            Neither thread can continue
                            until the circular dependency
                            is broken.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlock management helps maintain
                        system responsiveness, protects
                        shared data, and improves the
                        reliability of applications
                        that operate concurrently.
                    </p>
</section>
