<section class="chapter" id="chapter-25">
<h2>
                        25. Best Practices for Resource Allocation
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Effective resource allocation
                        helps operating systems and
                        applications use available
                        resources efficiently while
                        reducing the possibility
                        of deadlocks.
                    </p>
<h3>1. Use Consistent Resource Ordering</h3>
<p>
                        Processes should acquire
                        multiple resources in a
                        consistent predefined order.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This prevents circular
                        waiting relationships.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Release Resources Promptly</h3>
<p>
                        Processes should release
                        resources as soon as they
                        are no longer needed.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Holding resources unnecessarily
                        increases contention and
                        waiting time.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Minimize Lock Duration</h3>
<p>
                        Critical sections should be
                        kept as short as practical.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Shorter lock durations reduce
                        the time other processes
                        must wait.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Avoid Unnecessary Nested Locks</h3>
<p>
                        Acquiring several locks
                        simultaneously can increase
                        the complexity of resource
                        dependencies.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Applications should minimize
                        nested locking where possible.
                    </p>
<h3>5. Use Appropriate Timeouts</h3>
<p>
                        Timeouts can prevent applications
                        from waiting indefinitely for
                        certain resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        However, a timeout alone does
                        not eliminate every possible
                        deadlock.
                    </p>
<h3>6. Monitor Resource Usage</h3>
<p>
                        System monitoring tools can
                        help identify excessive
                        waiting times, lock contention,
                        and resource bottlenecks.
                    </p>
<h3>7. Apply Deadlock Detection</h3>
<p>
                        Systems that permit deadlocks
                        should use appropriate detection
                        and recovery mechanisms.
                    </p>
<h3>8. Design Safe Recovery Procedures</h3>
<p>
                        Applications should handle
                        transaction rollback,
                        resource cleanup, and
                        process termination carefully.
                    </p>
<h3>9. Test Concurrent Operations</h3>
<p>
                        Developers should test
                        applications under concurrent
                        workloads to identify
                        synchronization problems.
                    </p>
<h3>10. Use Suitable Synchronization Tools</h3>
<p>
                        Mutexes, semaphores, condition
                        variables, and other
                        synchronization mechanisms
                        should be used according
                        to their intended purposes.
