<h3>Summary</h3>
<p>
                        Deadlocks and resource allocation
                        are important concepts in
                        operating system management.
                    </p>
<p>
                        A deadlock occurs when processes
                        cannot continue because they
                        are waiting for resources
                        held by one another.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Four necessary conditions
                        contribute to deadlock:
                    </p>
<ol>
<li>Mutual Exclusion</li>
<li>Hold and Wait</li>
<li>No Preemption</li>
<li>Circular Wait</li>
</ol>
<p>
                        Resource Allocation Graphs
                        help illustrate relationships
                        between processes and resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Deadlock, starvation, and
                        livelock represent different
                        problems affecting process
                        execution.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Operating systems can manage
                        deadlocks using prevention,
                        avoidance, detection,
                        and recovery.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Deadlock prevention removes
                        at least one necessary
                        deadlock condition.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Deadlock avoidance evaluates
                        resource requests to keep
                        the system in a safe state.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The Banker's Algorithm is
                        an example of a deadlock
                        avoidance technique.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Deadlock detection identifies
                        existing deadlocks, while
                        recovery methods attempt
                        to restore system progress.
                    </p>
<h3>Key Lessons Learned</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                            Processes require resources
                            to perform their operations.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Improper resource allocation
                            can create deadlocks.
                        </li>
<li>
                            The four Coffman conditions
                            are necessary for traditional
                            resource deadlocks.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Resource Allocation Graphs
                            help visualize dependencies.
                        </li>
<li>
                            A safe state has at least
                            one safe process sequence.
                        </li>
<li>
                            The Banker's Algorithm
                            evaluates resource allocation
                            safety.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Detection and recovery
                            can resolve existing deadlocks.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Consistent resource ordering
                            helps prevent circular waits.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>
                        Understanding deadlocks and
                        resource allocation helps
                        Information Technology students
                        recognize how operating systems
                        coordinate shared resources.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Proper synchronization,
                        resource management,
                        and recovery strategies
                        support reliable and
                        efficient computer systems.
                    </p>
<p>
                        These concepts are especially
                        important in operating systems,
                        database management,
                        multithreaded programming,
                        and distributed applications.
